{
  "ID": "2.1",
  "Context/Background": "ASXL1 plays key roles in epigenetic regulation of early developmental gene expression. De novo protein-truncating mutations in ASXL1 cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), a rare neurodevelopmental condition characterized by severe intellectual disabilities, distinctive facial features, hypertrichosis, increased risk of Wilms tumor, and variable congenital anomalies, including heart defects and severe skeletal defects giving rise to a typical BOS posture. These BOS-causing ASXL1 variants are also high-prevalence somatic driver mutations in acute myeloid leukemia.  We used primary cells from individuals with BOS (n = 18) and controls (n = 49) to dissect gene regulatory changes caused by ASXL1 mutations using comprehensive multiomics assays for chromatin accessibility (ATAC-seq), DNA methylation, histone methylation binding, and transcriptome in peripheral blood and skin fibroblasts.\n",
  "Prompt": "\nBased on the results of the blood and fibroblast transcriptomic DEG analyses of BOS vs controls, provide a discussion of the common and distinct pathways found between blood and fibroblast and any relevance to the presence in the BOS samples of ASXL1 truncating mutations.",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 22:45:50",
  "elapsed_seconds": 96.58,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context**  \nASXL1 truncating mutations cause Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), characterized by neurodevelopmental defects, skeletal anomalies, and cancer predisposition. Transcriptomic analysis of BOS vs. controls in blood (n=18) and fibroblasts (n=49) revealed differentially expressed genes (DEGs) with potential relevance to ASXL1’s epigenetic regulatory role.  \n\n**Keywords**  \nASXL1, Bohring-Opitz syndrome (BOS), DEG, fibroblasts, blood, transcriptomic, pathway enrichment, truncating mutations.  \n\n**Candidate Genes**  \n- **Shared**: *VANGL2*, *GRIK5*  \n- **Fibroblast-specific**: *UGT3A2*, *TNMD*, *CNTN4*, *STMN2*  \n- **Blood-specific**: *SLC7A3*, *CFH*, *GREM2*  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments**  \nCounts_by_database analysis not performed (requires enrichment tool), but inferred themes from DEGs:  \n- **Fibroblasts (Upregulated)**: Cell adhesion molecules (CAMs), axon guidance (e.g., *CNTN4*, *VANGL2*), extracellular matrix (ECM) organization.  \n- **Fibroblasts (Downregulated)**: Microtubule dynamics (*STMN2*), neurodevelopmental pathways.  \n- **Blood (Upregulated)**: Immune response (*CFH* downregulation suggests complement dysregulation), amino acid transport (*SLC7A3*), Wnt signaling (*VANGL2*).  \n- **Blood (Downregulated)**: BMP antagonism (*GREM2*), ribosomal biogenesis (*RPL3L*).  \n\n**Targeted Subset**  \n- **ASXL1-related pathways**: Enrichment in ECM organization (fibroblasts) and immune dysregulation (blood) aligns with ASXL1’s role in chromatin remodeling and BOS phenotypes (e.g., skeletal defects, Wilms tumor risk).  \n- **Shared genes**: *VANGL2* (planar cell polarity) and *GRIK5* (neuronal excitability) link to BOS neurodevelopmental defects.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)**  \n| Gene      | Direction | Role/Function                     | Pathway(s)                |  \n|-----------|-----------|-----------------------------------|---------------------------|  \n| *VANGL2*  | Up        | Planar cell polarity                | Wnt signaling, ECM        |  \n| *GRIK5*   | Up        | Glutamate receptor subunit          | Neurotransmission         |  \n| *UGT3A2*  | Up        | Detoxification enzyme               | Metabolism                |  \n| *STMN2*  | Down      | Microtubule destabilization         | Neuronal development      |  \n| *CFH*    | Down      | Complement regulation               | Immune homeostasis        |  \n\n**Comparisons**  \n- **Shared**: *VANGL2* and *GRIK5* upregulation suggests conserved dysregulation in neurodevelopmental pathways.  \n- **Tissue-specific**: Fibroblasts show ECM/microtubule changes, while blood highlights immune/metabolic shifts. Discordant findings (e.g., *CFH* down in blood only) may reflect tissue-specific ASXL1 targets.  \n\n**Implications**  \nASXL1 truncations likely disrupt chromatin accessibility in a tissue-specific manner, driving:  \n1. **Fibroblasts**: ECM dysregulation contributing to skeletal defects.  \n2. **Blood**: Complement activation and metabolic shifts, potentially linking to tumor risk.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification**  \n1. Validate *VANGL2* and *GRIK5* expression in ASXL1-mutant models to confirm their role in BOS pathogenesis.  \n2. Perform ATAC-seq integration to identify ASXL1 binding sites near DEGs, confirming direct epigenetic regulation.  \n3. Investigate whether *CFH* downregulation correlates with complement activation in BOS patients’ blood.  \n\n**References**  \nNone validated in this analysis.  \n\n---  \n*Note: Pathway enrichment analysis and OMIM validation require additional tools not used here.*",
